# Line-ending policy: LF everywhere, in the repository and in the working tree.
#
# Before this file existed the repository had mixed endings - 49 tracked text
# files were stored with CRLF and the rest with LF, sometimes within the same
# directory. That made `git diff --cached --check` report every added line of a
# CRLF file as trailing whitespace, which trains you to ignore the check that
# exists to catch real problems.
#
# `text=auto` lets git detect text vs binary rather than trusting this list to
# be exhaustive; `eol=lf` then pins the checkout to LF on every platform,
# including Windows. Renormalized in one dedicated commit so the change is
# separable from behavioural history.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ── Explicit text types ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Redundant with text=auto, but stated so a future binary-looking heuristic
# miss cannot silently corrupt a source file.
*.kt      text
*.kts     text
*.java    text
*.xml     text
*.md      text
*.pro     text
*.toml    text
*.properties text
*.ndjson  text
*.json    text
*.txt     text

# ── Windows batch stays CRLF ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Deliberate exception to the LF rule. cmd.exe has known failure modes parsing
# LF-only batch files - notably `goto` and label handling - and gradlew.bat is
# the build entry point on this machine. The risk of a subtly broken build
# script outweighs uniformity here. Remove this line if you want literal
# all-LF and are willing to test the wrapper.
*.bat     text eol=crlf
*.cmd     text eol=crlf

# ── Binary: never normalize, never diff as text ──────────────────────────────
*.jar     binary
*.aab     binary
*.apk     binary
*.keystore binary
*.jks     binary
*.png     binary
*.webp    binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.ico     binary
*.pdf     binary
*.zip     binary
*.pcapng  binary
